% TODO: manual review -- [CLAUDE 2026-10-10] rewritten on the pattern of the reference abstracts (problem first, then "First, ... Next, ... Last, ..." in chapter order, in "we"). Six paragraphs, no results numbers. Same order and terms as Chapter 1's outline and Chapter 8.
% TODO: manual review -- [CLAUDE 2026-10-10, second pass] cut from 534 to under 300 words to meet the ABSTRACT LIMIT above; same order and claims, phrasing on Buttenschoen's abstract ("This thesis investigates ... First ... Next ... Last").
This thesis investigates label noise in quantitative structure--activity relationship (QSAR) models, whose labels carry measurement error, differences between laboratories and assay limits. Most studies of label noise in QSAR assume Gaussian noise and study representations or models in isolation. We show that the kind of noise matters, and that noise can be handled once the features suit the data.

First, we introduced NoiseInject, an open-source package for benchmarking noise robustness. Across 13 base models, six representations and seven types of label noise, model family decided robustness more than the representation did; random forests were the most consistent model. Systematic noise, such as labels clipped at an assay limit, did the most damage. Predicted uncertainty rose with noise but, outside censoring, did not identify the corrupted labels.

Next, we traced why published methods for learning with noisy labels did poorly on molecular data. Within one dataset, most features of a standard representation barely vary or do not relate to the label, yet those that vary still add to the distance between molecules. Distance-based methods therefore inherit the problem.

We then built the adaptive hybrid representation, which selects features from several representations for each dataset and model. On every small-molecule dataset, though not on the reactions, it predicted better than the best single representation, and its distances tracked activity more closely. A newly published embedding can be added as one more representation.

Last, we compared single representations and hybrids under label noise. Noise was handled, mostly by methods built or adapted for molecular data. Which method worked depended on the task, the data, the model and the noise, but on small molecules the hybrid stayed ahead in almost every condition.

The noise in a molecular dataset should therefore be modeled before a method is chosen, and the features adapted to each dataset.

